pushFwd(RingMap,Module) -- push forward of a module via a finite ring map
*************************************************************************

  * Function: *note pushFwd: pushFwd,
  * Usage: 
        N = pushFwd(f, M)
        N = pushFwd M
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring map: (Macaulay2Doc)RingMap,, from a ring $A$ to a ring
        $B$ or the natural map from coefficientRing B if f not specified
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, a $B$-module, which via $f$
        is a finite $A$-module
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note NoPrune: NoPrune, => ..., default value false, NoPrune option for
        pushFwd
  * Outputs:
      * N, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, 

Description
===========

Given a (not necessarily finite) ring map $f : A \to B$, the $B$-module $M$ can
be considered as a module over $A$. If $M$ is finite, this method returns the
corresponding $A$-module.

+--------------------------------+
|i1 : kk = QQ;                   |
+--------------------------------+
|i2 : A = kk[t];                 |
+--------------------------------+
|i3 : B = kk[x,y]/(x*y);         |
+--------------------------------+
|i4 : use B;                     |
+--------------------------------+
|i5 : i = ideal(x)               |
|                                |
|o5 = ideal x                    |
|                                |
|o5 : Ideal of B                 |
+--------------------------------+
|i6 : f = map(B,A,{x})           |
|                                |
|o6 = map (B, A, {x})            |
|                                |
|o6 : RingMap B <-- A            |
+--------------------------------+
|i7 : pushFwd(f,module i)        |
|                                |
|      1                         |
|o7 = A                          |
|                                |
|o7 : A-module, free, degrees {1}|
+--------------------------------+

